1. Movies vs. TV shows
Finding: Netflix's 8,807 titles are about 70% movies (6,131) and 30% TV shows (2,676), so movies outnumber shows roughly 2.3 to 1.
Why it matters: The catalog leans toward movies, but TV shows tend to keep viewers subscribed longer.
Recommendation: Netflix could invest more in series, especially in the genres and countries that already perform well.

2. Content growth
Finding: Content rose sharply from about 2015, peaked around 2018–2019 at roughly 2,000 titles, then fell to about 1,500 by the last year.
Why it matters: Growth in new titles has slowed from its peak, which may mean a shift toward quality over volume.
Recommendation: Check whether the last year is incomplete before claiming a decline, and say whether the axis shows release year or the year added to Netflix.

3. Top countries
Finding: The United States leads by a wide margin (about 3,600 titles), followed by India (about 1,000).
Why it matters: Netflix's catalog is concentrated in two markets, and the U.S. has more than three times as many titles as India.
Recommendation: There's room to grow in other countries, especially fast-growing streaming markets.

4. Ratings
Finding: TV-MA is the most common rating, followed by TV-14, so the catalog is mainly aimed at adults and teens.
Why it matters: Content for younger children is a small share of the catalog.
Recommendation: Kids' content could be an expansion opportunity.

5. Genres
Finding: Documentaries, drama and stand-up comedy are among the top genres.
Why it matters: These formats are cheaper to produce than large scripted productions and often travel well across countries.
Recommendation: Keep investing in documentaries and stand-up while expanding in less-covered genres.

6. Movie duration
Finding: Movie length is bell-shaped, peaking around 90–100 minutes (average 100), and few movies run longer than 150 minutes.
Why it matters: Most movies follow the standard feature-length format.
Recommendation: Content planning can target the 90–110 minute range.
